1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,750
Welcome to Code Quest, Chapter 1.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:07,886
Today you will write your first Python
program, and you will learn to work with

3
00:00:07,906 --> 00:00:08,920
an AI assistant.

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:10,219
Meet Asha.

5
00:00:10,239 --> 00:00:13,670
She helps at her family's cafe, the Corner
Cafe.

6
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,340
Her mission today is to make the computer
print this receipt.

7
00:00:17,367 --> 00:00:21,772
By the end of this video, you will
understand every line of this program, and

8
00:00:21,792 --> 00:00:23,957
you will be able to build it yourself.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,136
Along the way, an AI assistant will be
confidently wrong.

10
00:00:28,156 --> 00:00:29,886
Your job is to catch it.

11
00:00:30,483 --> 00:00:31,517
Here is the plan.

12
00:00:31,537 --> 00:00:33,873
Each goal is one piece of the mission.

13
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,770
Run a Python file and see its output.

14
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,650
Explain how Python runs a program, from
top to bottom.

15
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,980
Control spacing with print, sep and end.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,370
Read an error message and find the line
that caused it.

17
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:52,270
And use an AI assistant at every stage,
safely, while you check its answers.

18
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:56,960
A program is a list of instructions that a
computer follows exactly.

19
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,427
The till at Asha's cafe runs one.

20
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:02,320
So do train timetables and calculators.

21
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:06,655
AI assistants such as ChatGPT and Claude
can write code.

22
00:01:06,675 --> 00:01:08,271
They are often right.

23
00:01:08,291 --> 00:01:11,887
Sometimes they are wrong, and still sound
sure.

24
00:01:11,933 --> 00:01:16,007
So you are the one who runs the code, and
decides if it is correct.

25
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:18,297
Here is the big idea.

26
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:22,673
A Python program is a text file that ends
in dot p y.

27
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:27,664
Python, the program called the
interpreter, reads it and carries out each

28
00:01:27,684 --> 00:01:31,009
line in order: top to bottom, one line at
a time.

29
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:35,627
Print is a function: a named action that
Python already knows.

30
00:01:35,647 --> 00:01:38,290
You call it with its name, then brackets.

31
00:01:38,333 --> 00:01:41,777
And text inside quotation marks is called
a string.

32
00:01:42,387 --> 00:01:45,157
Think of a recipe card in Asha's kitchen.

33
00:01:45,177 --> 00:01:47,607
The cook follows each step in order.

34
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:49,985
The comparison stops at one point.

35
00:01:50,005 --> 00:01:52,217
A cook can guess what you meant.

36
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:53,193
Python cannot.

37
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:57,040
One typing mistake, and Python stops with
an error.

38
00:01:57,653 --> 00:01:59,058
Let's check that idea.

39
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:00,935
A file has three print lines.

40
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:03,265
In which order does Python run them?

41
00:02:14,647 --> 00:02:14,782
B.

42
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:17,814
From top to bottom, one line at a time.

43
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:19,884
Take a short breather.

44
00:02:19,904 --> 00:02:21,840
Here is what you know so far.

45
00:02:21,867 --> 00:02:26,108
Next, Asha installs Python and writes her
very first line.

46
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:32,678
Step 1: install Python.

47
00:02:32,698 --> 00:02:34,525
Get the latest version.

48
00:02:34,545 --> 00:02:38,060
Any version from 3.12 works for this
course.

49
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:43,629
On Windows, use the Python install manager
from python dot org or the Microsoft

50
00:02:43,649 --> 00:02:44,050
Store.

51
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:49,170
On macOS, download the installer package
from python dot org.

52
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,565
On Linux, Python is usually installed
already.

53
00:02:53,187 --> 00:02:56,847
Then open a terminal and check the
version.

54
00:02:56,867 --> 00:02:59,897
On Windows, type python, space, dash dash
version.

55
00:02:59,933 --> 00:03:03,180
On macOS and Linux, type python3 instead.

56
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:07,003
You should see a line that starts with
Python 3.

57
00:03:07,583 --> 00:03:08,108
Step 2.

58
00:03:08,128 --> 00:03:14,336
Asha opens a code editor, such as Visual
Studio Code or IDLE, and creates a file

59
00:03:14,356 --> 00:03:15,893
called ex 0 1 hello.

60
00:03:15,933 --> 00:03:19,597
Line 1 starts with a hash sign, so it is a
comment.

61
00:03:19,617 --> 00:03:20,897
Python ignores it.

62
00:03:20,917 --> 00:03:23,063
Comments are notes for people.

63
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,970
Line 2 prints a greeting with her name.

64
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,153
Line 3 prints a second line.

65
00:03:28,753 --> 00:03:29,801
Step 3: run it.

66
00:03:29,821 --> 00:03:33,643
In the terminal, go to the folder that
holds the file.

67
00:03:33,667 --> 00:03:36,627
On Windows, type python, then the file
name.

68
00:03:36,667 --> 00:03:41,216
On macOS and Linux, type python3, then the
file name.

69
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:43,759
And there it is: two lines.

70
00:03:43,779 --> 00:03:46,370
Each print makes one line of output.

71
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,589
The quotation marks do not appear.

72
00:03:48,609 --> 00:03:51,578
They only mark where the text starts and
ends.

73
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:52,703
Step 4.

74
00:03:52,723 --> 00:03:56,548
Asha writes the cafe's tea recipe as a
program.

75
00:03:56,568 --> 00:03:58,430
Step 1, boil the water.

76
00:03:58,467 --> 00:04:00,537
Step 2, add the tea leaves.

77
00:04:00,567 --> 00:04:03,207
Step 3, pour the tea into a cup.

78
00:04:03,233 --> 00:04:05,675
You know how Python reads a file.

79
00:04:05,695 --> 00:04:06,645
Use that now.

80
00:04:18,013 --> 00:04:20,018
The same order as the code.

81
00:04:20,038 --> 00:04:21,743
Step 1, step 2, step 3.

82
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:25,217
Swap two lines in your file and run it
again.

83
00:04:25,237 --> 00:04:30,306
The output changes, because Python always
runs from top to bottom.

84
00:04:30,883 --> 00:04:31,368
Step 5.

85
00:04:31,388 --> 00:04:35,695
The receipt needs neat spacing, so Asha
learns two settings.

86
00:04:35,715 --> 00:04:39,373
Print can show several values, separated
by commas.

87
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,201
Sep sets what goes between the values.

88
00:04:42,221 --> 00:04:44,280
By default, it is one space.

89
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,234
End sets what goes after the last value.

90
00:04:47,254 --> 00:04:49,376
By default, it is a new line.

91
00:04:49,987 --> 00:04:55,207
Line 2 has a calculation, 3 plus 4, with
no quotation marks.

92
00:04:55,233 --> 00:05:00,213
Line 3 prints the cafe's opening time, and
sets sep to a colon.

93
00:05:00,233 --> 00:05:05,010
Line 4 sets end to three dots, and line 5
prints the word done.

94
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:21,830
Line 2 prints 7, because Python calculates
anything without quotation marks.

95
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:26,357
Line 3 joins the values with colons, so
they look like a time.

96
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:32,569
And line 4 ends with three dots instead of
a new line, so line 5 continues on the

97
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:35,472
same line: Loading, dot dot dot, done.

98
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:36,802
Quick check.

99
00:05:36,822 --> 00:05:39,928
Which setting changes what goes between
the values?

100
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:51,899
B, sep.

101
00:05:51,919 --> 00:05:53,451
Sep goes between.

102
00:05:53,471 --> 00:05:54,821
End goes after.

103
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:55,999
Breather.

104
00:05:56,019 --> 00:05:57,395
Relax your shoulders.

105
00:05:57,415 --> 00:05:59,790
Look at how much you can already do.

106
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:02,847
Now Asha has every piece she needs for the
receipt.

107
00:06:07,583 --> 00:06:09,925
Remember the receipt from the start?

108
00:06:09,945 --> 00:06:11,761
Now you can read every line.

109
00:06:11,781 --> 00:06:14,713
Lines 2 to 4 print the header, top to
bottom.

110
00:06:14,733 --> 00:06:19,953
Lines 5 and 6 use sep to draw the dots
between each item and its price.

111
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,630
Lines 7 and 8 print the item count and the
total.

112
00:06:23,667 --> 00:06:24,946
Asha runs it.

113
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:38,481
Items 2, and Total U S D 10.

114
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:41,683
Python worked out 1 plus 1, and 3 plus 7.

115
00:06:41,733 --> 00:06:43,160
The receipt works.

116
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:45,168
But it has no order code.

117
00:06:45,188 --> 00:06:51,998
Asha wants to add one, and she will use an
AI assistant to help, one stage at a time.

118
00:06:52,583 --> 00:06:57,063
Before Asha opens an AI assistant, she
follows three safety habits.

119
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:57,397
One.

120
00:06:57,417 --> 00:07:01,440
Never paste passwords, keys or private
information.

121
00:07:01,467 --> 00:07:01,744
Two.

122
00:07:01,764 --> 00:07:07,397
Run every piece of AI code yourself, and
compare it with what you asked for.

123
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:07,862
Three.

124
00:07:07,882 --> 00:07:12,720
You are responsible for the code you use,
even if an AI wrote it.

125
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:14,324
Stage 1: plan.

126
00:07:14,344 --> 00:07:18,202
Before any code, Asha asks how to add the
order code.

127
00:07:18,222 --> 00:07:21,640
She says what she wants, and says: no code
yet.

128
00:07:21,667 --> 00:07:24,143
The AI answers with four clear steps.

129
00:07:24,163 --> 00:07:26,571
Each one is something she can check.

130
00:07:26,591 --> 00:07:30,754
A plan you understand is easier to check
than code you do not.

131
00:07:31,353 --> 00:07:32,342
Stage 2: write.

132
00:07:32,362 --> 00:07:37,385
Before she changes her receipt, Asha makes
sure she understands every line.

133
00:07:37,405 --> 00:07:41,083
She asks the AI to explain her code, not
to rewrite it.

134
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:46,778
The AI explains each group, and shows
where the order code belongs: after line

135
00:07:46,798 --> 00:07:46,923
3.

136
00:07:46,943 --> 00:07:50,627
Asha checks each sentence against what she
learned.

137
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:52,531
Stage 3: generate.

138
00:07:52,551 --> 00:07:58,448
Now Asha asks for the code line itself:
one line that prints the order code with

139
00:07:58,468 --> 00:07:59,040
hyphens.

140
00:07:59,067 --> 00:08:04,317
The AI gives a line of code, and says it
prints the order code with hyphens.

141
00:08:04,337 --> 00:08:05,357
It sounds sure.

142
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:06,563
Look at its code.

143
00:08:06,583 --> 00:08:10,042
You learned exactly what this line does in
step 5.

144
00:08:21,413 --> 00:08:22,362
Asha runs it.

145
00:08:22,382 --> 00:08:24,597
It prints spaces, not hyphens.

146
00:08:24,617 --> 00:08:27,428
The AI was wrong, and it sounded sure.

147
00:08:27,448 --> 00:08:28,992
Can she find out why?

148
00:08:29,012 --> 00:08:31,078
That is what testing is for.

149
00:08:31,687 --> 00:08:32,750
Stage 4: test.

150
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:38,167
Asha gives the AI what she expected, and
what the line really printed.

151
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:43,864
This time the AI finds both differences,
and names the cause: sep is a space by

152
00:08:43,884 --> 00:08:44,439
default.

153
00:08:44,459 --> 00:08:46,310
It adds sep equals hyphen.

154
00:08:46,333 --> 00:08:48,134
Asha does not trust it yet.

155
00:08:48,154 --> 00:08:48,943
She runs it.

156
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:53,055
Now the order code is correct, so she adds
it to her receipt.

157
00:08:53,653 --> 00:08:54,628
Stage 5: debug.

158
00:08:54,648 --> 00:08:59,935
While adding the order code, Asha types
Print with a capital P on the last line.

159
00:08:59,955 --> 00:09:01,726
Python stops with an error.

160
00:09:01,746 --> 00:09:04,313
She pastes her code and the full error.

161
00:09:04,333 --> 00:09:08,281
She asks for the cause first, then the
smallest fix.

162
00:09:08,301 --> 00:09:14,155
The AI names line 11, explains that names
are case-sensitive, and changes one

163
00:09:14,175 --> 00:09:14,689
letter.

164
00:09:15,287 --> 00:09:16,460
Stage 6: improve.

165
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:18,004
The receipt now works.

166
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:22,917
Asha asks for at most two readability
suggestions, each with a reason.

167
00:09:22,967 --> 00:09:24,411
Suggestion 1 helps.

168
00:09:24,431 --> 00:09:27,647
Suggestion 2 says the dots do not line up.

169
00:09:27,667 --> 00:09:29,286
Asha checks it herself.

170
00:09:29,306 --> 00:09:34,347
She counts: both price rows are 23
characters, so they already line up.

171
00:09:34,367 --> 00:09:35,986
The AI was wrong again.

172
00:09:36,006 --> 00:09:39,550
She keeps suggestion 1 only, and runs the
receipt.

173
00:09:40,153 --> 00:09:41,512
Here is the result.

174
00:09:41,532 --> 00:09:45,357
A comment that says what the program does,
on line 2.

175
00:09:45,377 --> 00:09:47,533
And the order code, on line 5.

176
00:09:47,567 --> 00:09:52,328
Every stage helped, and at every stage
Asha checked the answer herself.

177
00:09:52,348 --> 00:09:54,281
That is how you work with AI.

178
00:09:54,887 --> 00:09:56,915
Quick check on what you watched.

179
00:09:56,935 --> 00:09:59,027
Your program stops with an error.

180
00:09:59,047 --> 00:10:00,499
Which stage do you use?

181
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:11,999
C.

182
00:10:12,019 --> 00:10:12,418
Debug.

183
00:10:12,438 --> 00:10:16,809
Paste the code and the full error, and ask
for the cause first.

184
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:18,047
Breather.

185
00:10:18,067 --> 00:10:23,293
You watched Asha use AI at every stage,
and she caught two AI mistakes by

186
00:10:23,313 --> 00:10:23,940
checking.

187
00:10:23,967 --> 00:10:29,098
Next, the four errors every beginner
meets, including the one Asha debugged.

188
00:10:33,850 --> 00:10:34,502
Mistake 1.

189
00:10:34,522 --> 00:10:39,880
The closing quotation mark is missing, so
Python cannot see where the text ends.

190
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:41,776
It reports a syntax error.

191
00:10:41,796 --> 00:10:46,590
That means Python could not read the file,
so no line runs at all.

192
00:10:46,633 --> 00:10:49,419
The fix: add the closing quotation mark.

193
00:10:49,439 --> 00:10:54,960
Remember this rule: every opening mark
needs its closing partner, for quotation

194
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:56,573
marks and for brackets.

195
00:10:57,187 --> 00:11:00,580
Mistake 2 is the one Asha debugged with
the AI.

196
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:03,703
Print with a capital P is a different
name.

197
00:11:03,723 --> 00:11:06,027
Here it is in a short test file.

198
00:11:06,067 --> 00:11:08,063
Notice that Start printed first.

199
00:11:08,083 --> 00:11:13,355
This error happened while the program was
running, so the lines above it had already

200
00:11:13,375 --> 00:11:13,607
run.

201
00:11:13,633 --> 00:11:18,737
The report names line 3, and the last line
even suggests the fix.

202
00:11:19,353 --> 00:11:20,072
Mistake 3.

203
00:11:20,092 --> 00:11:26,053
Word processors and some chat apps turn
straight quotation marks into curly ones.

204
00:11:26,073 --> 00:11:28,563
Python accepts only straight ones.

205
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:34,184
So when you copy code from a chat, copy it
from the code block, never from formatted

206
00:11:34,204 --> 00:11:34,518
text.

207
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,238
Now you know three errors, and the rule
about closing partners.

208
00:11:39,258 --> 00:11:40,815
Try the fourth yourself.

209
00:11:52,180 --> 00:11:53,543
The closing bracket.

210
00:11:53,563 --> 00:11:56,725
Every opening bracket needs a closing
bracket.

211
00:11:57,320 --> 00:11:58,474
Here are all four.

212
00:11:58,494 --> 00:12:02,060
Each row starts with the last line of its
error report.

213
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:06,360
Unterminated string literal: a quotation
mark is missing.

214
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:10,680
Name is not defined: check the spelling,
such as a capital P.

215
00:12:10,700 --> 00:12:13,930
Invalid character: retype the quotation
marks.

216
00:12:13,967 --> 00:12:16,807
Was never closed: add the closing bracket.

217
00:12:16,833 --> 00:12:18,301
So the habit is simple.

218
00:12:18,321 --> 00:12:21,665
Read the last line first, then find the
line number.

219
00:12:22,287 --> 00:12:23,042
Quick check.

220
00:12:23,062 --> 00:12:25,237
An error report has several lines.

221
00:12:25,257 --> 00:12:27,046
Which one do you read first?

222
00:12:38,413 --> 00:12:38,540
B.

223
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:39,566
The last line.

224
00:12:39,586 --> 00:12:41,545
It names the kind of error.

225
00:12:42,153 --> 00:12:43,444
One more breather.

226
00:12:43,464 --> 00:12:46,211
Every developer sees errors every day.

227
00:12:46,231 --> 00:12:47,813
Now you can read them.

228
00:12:47,833 --> 00:12:51,029
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

229
00:12:55,750 --> 00:12:56,122
First.

230
00:12:56,142 --> 00:13:00,170
The cafe wants the order code written
backwards, with slashes.

231
00:13:00,190 --> 00:13:02,913
What would you change in Asha's code line?

232
00:13:14,280 --> 00:13:19,003
Try it in your editor after the video, and
compare the output with the target,

233
00:13:19,023 --> 00:13:20,401
character by character.

234
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:21,457
Second.

235
00:13:21,477 --> 00:13:24,394
An AI gives you code that runs with no
error.

236
00:13:24,414 --> 00:13:27,592
What two things do you check before you
trust it?

237
00:13:38,947 --> 00:13:40,008
Keep your answer.

238
00:13:40,028 --> 00:13:42,106
You will use it in every chapter.

239
00:13:42,683 --> 00:13:44,023
Let's recap the mission.

240
00:13:44,067 --> 00:13:48,297
Python runs a program from top to bottom,
one line at a time.

241
00:13:48,333 --> 00:13:53,184
Print shows text and numbers, and
calculations without quotation marks are

242
00:13:53,204 --> 00:13:54,303
worked out first.

243
00:13:54,333 --> 00:13:57,973
Sep goes between values, and end goes
after them.

244
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,520
Read the last line of an error first.

245
00:14:00,567 --> 00:14:05,147
And use AI at every stage, then run and
check everything yourself.

246
00:14:05,753 --> 00:14:06,962
One last check.

247
00:14:06,982 --> 00:14:10,401
Asha wants a new line: Tea, dash, U S D 3.

248
00:14:10,421 --> 00:14:12,120
Which code prints it?

249
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:23,604
A.

250
00:14:23,624 --> 00:14:26,639
The separator goes between the two values.

251
00:14:27,253 --> 00:14:28,604
Mission complete.

252
00:14:28,624 --> 00:14:31,990
You earned 100 XP and the First Run badge.

253
00:14:34,217 --> 00:14:36,006
Now it is your turn to build.

254
00:14:36,026 --> 00:14:38,127
The practice file has eight tasks.

255
00:14:38,167 --> 00:14:41,197
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

256
00:14:41,233 --> 00:14:44,413
Three apply tasks, worth 20 XP each.

257
00:14:44,433 --> 00:14:46,931
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

258
00:14:46,951 --> 00:14:50,673
In the last one, you check an AI's work,
like Asha did.

259
00:14:50,700 --> 00:14:53,312
Try each task before you open the
solution.

260
00:14:53,883 --> 00:14:56,814
Asha typed every price twice on her
receipt.

261
00:14:56,834 --> 00:15:02,046
In Chapter 2, you will fix that with
variables: store a value once, and use it

262
00:15:02,066 --> 00:15:02,783
many times.

263
00:15:02,833 --> 00:15:04,573
Do the warm-up tasks now.

264
00:15:04,593 --> 00:15:06,543
They take about ten minutes.

265
00:15:06,567 --> 00:15:08,212
See you in Chapter 2.
